        <div id="merv" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. MERV Ratings Explained: What MERV Should a Furnace Filter Be?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The best furnace filter is the highest MERV rating your system handles comfortably, usually MERV 8 to 13 for a 1-inch filter. MERV, or Minimum Efficiency Reporting Value, rates how well a filter captures particles; higher numbers catch smaller particles.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">MERV 8: Basic Protection</h3>

          <p>
            MERV 8 catches dust, lint and pollen and protects the furnace with minimal airflow restriction. Filterbuy says its MERV 8 captures 90 percent of dust. It suits homes without allergies or pets.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">MERV 11: The Sweet Spot</h3>

          <p>
            MERV 11, like the Filtrete MPR 1000 and K&N washable, adds pet dander and finer dust. For most homes it is the best balance of filtration and airflow.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">MERV 12 and 13: High Filtration</h3>

          <p>
            MERV 12 and 13 capture smaller particles, including smoke and many allergens. Nordic Pure and Aerostar both list their MERV 12 and 13 filters as equivalent to Filtrete MPR 1500 to 1900.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">MPR and FPR Are Brand Scales</h3>

          <p>
            Filtrete uses MPR and Honeywell uses FPR. The listings here map them roughly: MERV 8 is about MPR 700 or FPR 5, MERV 11 is MPR 1000, and MERV 13 is about MPR 1500 to 1900 or FPR 10.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="airflow" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Higher MERV vs Airflow: Can a Filter Hurt Your Furnace?
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the most important question in choosing a furnace filter.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why Airflow Matters</h3>

          <p>
            Your furnace blower is designed to move a certain amount of air. A filter that restricts too much makes the system work harder, can reduce heating and cooling, and in some systems can cause overheating or frozen AC coils.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What Most Systems Handle</h3>

          <p>
            Most modern residential systems handle MERV 8 to 11 easily, and many handle MERV 13. Older systems or those already struggling may not. Check your HVAC manual, or ask a technician, before going above MERV 11.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">A Clogged Filter Is Worse Than a High MERV</h3>

          <p>
            A dirty filter restricts airflow far more than a clean high-MERV one. Changing on schedule matters more than which MERV you choose.
          </p>

