        <div id="size" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How to Find the Right Furnace Filter Size
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buying the wrong size is the most common furnace filter mistake.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Read the Old Filter</h3>

          <p>
            The size is printed on the frame of your current filter, usually as length x width x thickness, such as 16x25x1. Buy that exact size.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Nominal vs Actual Size</h3>

          <p>
            Filter sizes are nominal. A 16x25x1 filter actually measures around 15.5 x 24.5 x 0.75 inches; the listings here range from 15.50 x 24.50 (Filterbuy) to 15.68 x 24.68 (Filtrete). That is normal and lets the filter slide in.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">1-Inch vs 4- or 5-Inch Filters</h3>

          <p>
            Some systems use thicker media filters. These hold more dust, last longer and restrict airflow less at the same MERV. Never force a 1-inch filter into a 4-inch slot; buy the correct thickness.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-often" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How Often Should You Change a Furnace Filter?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every filter maker here gives a maximum, and many homes need to change sooner.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The 90-Day Baseline</h3>

          <p>
            Filtrete, Filterbuy and Aerostar all list about three months for their 1-inch pleated filters. Filtrete says to change at least every 90 days.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When to Change Sooner</h3>

          <p>
            Pets, allergies, construction dust, a smoker in the home or heavy heating and cooling seasons all fill filters faster. Many households with pets change every 30 to 60 days.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Check It Monthly</h3>

          <p>
            Pull the filter and hold it up to a light. If you cannot see light through it, change it. Filtrete's Smart App can set 30-, 60- or 90-day reminders.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="allergies-pets" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. The Best Furnace Filter for Allergies and Pets
          </h2>

          <p>
            Allergy and pet owners benefit most from a better filter, but the filter is one part of the solution.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Choose MERV 11 to 13</h3>

          <p>
            MERV 11 captures much more pet dander than MERV 8, and MERV 13, like the Filtrete MPR 1900 or Aerostar, captures finer allergens. Both Filtrete filters here are certified asthma and allergy friendly, per their listings.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Run the Fan</h3>

          <p>
            A furnace filter only cleans air that passes through it. Running the HVAC fan more often, or on a circulate setting, filters more air.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Add an Air Purifier Where It Matters</h3>

          <p>
            A portable HEPA air purifier in the bedroom catches finer particles than any 1-inch furnace filter. See our guides to the <a href="/best-air-purifier-for-allergies/">best air purifiers for allergies</a> and <a href="/best-air-purifier-for-home/">air purifiers for home</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="reusable" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Reusable vs Disposable Furnace Filters
          </h2>

          <p>
            Washable filters appeal to anyone tired of buying filters, but the trade-offs are real.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Case for Washable</h3>

          <p>
            A washable filter like the K&N MERV 11 is a one-time purchase. K&N lists savings of about $30 a year versus disposables, and you never run out of filters.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Case for Disposable</h3>

          <p>
            Disposable filters are swapped in seconds, with no washing or drying, and the filter is always fresh. Most people find them easier to keep up with.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Drying Time Matters</h3>

          <p>
            A washable filter must be completely dry before refitting, because a damp filter can grow mold and lets the media sag. Many owners keep a second filter to swap while one dries.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="install" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. How to Change a Furnace Filter
          </h2>

          <p>
            Changing a filter takes two minutes.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 1: Turn the System Off</h3>

          <p>
            Switch the thermostat or furnace off so the blower is not pulling dust past the open slot.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 2: Remove the Old Filter</h3>

          <p>
            The filter usually sits in a slot beside the furnace or behind a return-air grille. Note the size printed on it and the direction of the airflow arrow.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 3: Point the Arrow Toward the Furnace</h3>

          <p>
            Every filter has an airflow arrow. It should point toward the furnace, in the direction air flows, away from the return duct.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 4: Write the Date and Turn It Back On</h3>

          <p>
            Write the install date on the frame, close the slot or grille and turn the system back on.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="smoke-odor" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Furnace Filters for Smoke, Mold and Odors
          </h2>

          <p>
            Furnace filters help with smoke and mold spores, but have limits.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Smoke</h3>

          <p>
            MERV 13 is commonly recommended for smoke where a system can handle it. The Aerostar and Filtrete MPR 1900 are both MERV 13. For heavy smoke, a portable purifier helps more; see the <a href="/best-air-purifier-for-smoke/">best air purifiers for smoke</a>.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Mold Spores</h3>

          <p>
            MERV 11 to 13 filters capture many mold spores in circulating air, but they do not fix the moisture source. Our guide to whether <a href="/does-a-hepa-filter-remove-mold-spores/">a HEPA filter removes mold spores</a> and the <a href="/best-dehumidifier-for-basement/">basement dehumidifier guide</a> cover the root cause.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Odors</h3>

          <p>
            Only carbon removes odors. The Nordic Pure Plus Carbon adds a thin carbon layer for everyday household smells.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="filter-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Fiberglass, Pleated, Electrostatic and Media Filters
          </h2>

          <p>
            Furnace filters come in a few constructions, and the type affects filtration as much as the rating.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Fiberglass Filters</h3>

          <p>
            Thin fiberglass panels are the cheapest filters and mainly protect the blower from large debris. Filterbuy says its pleated MERV 8 lasts three times longer than fiberglass models. They do little for air quality.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Pleated Filters</h3>

          <p>
            Pleats add surface area, so the filter catches more without clogging as quickly. Every disposable filter on this list is pleated, which is why they last up to about three months.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Electrostatic Filters</h3>

          <p>
            Electrostatically charged media, like the Filterbuy and Aerostar filters, attract particles as air passes through, improving capture without making the media denser. Some washable filters also use electrostatic designs.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Thick Media Filters</h3>

          <p>
            Four- and five-inch media filters fit dedicated cabinets on some systems. They hold far more dust and restrict airflow less than a 1-inch filter of the same MERV. If your system has one, buy the matching thickness.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="heat-pumps-ac" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            11. Furnace Filters for Heat Pumps and Central AC
          </h2>

          <p>
            The same filter serves heating and cooling in most homes, which is why changing it on schedule matters year-round.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">One Filter, Two Seasons</h3>

          <p>
            Central AC and heat pumps usually share the furnace or air handler blower and filter. Filtrete, Filterbuy and Aerostar all describe their filters for furnaces, air conditioners and heat pumps.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why Airflow Matters More for Cooling</h3>

          <p>
            A restricted filter can reduce airflow across the AC coil, which in some systems causes the coil to ice up. A clean filter of the right MERV prevents this.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Ductless Systems Are Different</h3>

          <p>
            Ductless mini splits have their own washable filters inside each indoor unit and do not use furnace filters. See our <a href="/best-mini-split-heat-pump/">mini split heat pump guide</a> for their care.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="yearly-cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            12. What Furnace Filters Cost Over a Year
          </h2>

          <p>
            Filter cost is mostly about how many you use, not the price of one.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Count Your Changes</h3>

          <p>
            At one filter every 90 days, you need four a year; at every 60 days with pets, six; at every 30 days, twelve. Multi-packs, like the six- and 12-packs sold by Filterbuy, Nordic Pure and Aerostar, cut the cost per filter.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Higher MERV Costs More</h3>

          <p>
            MERV 13 filters cost more per filter than MERV 8. Weigh that against whether anyone in the home needs the extra filtration.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Washable Payback</h3>

          <p>
            A washable filter costs more up front and pays back over years. K&N lists savings of about $30 a year versus disposables; the real figure depends on what you spend now.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="renters-mobile-homes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            13. Furnace Filters for Apartments and Mobile Homes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Renters and mobile home owners often have smaller systems and unusual filter locations, so a few points are worth knowing.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Apartments and Rentals</h3>

          <p>
            Many apartments have the filter behind a return grille in a hallway or ceiling. Check your lease: some landlords supply filters or change them, and others expect tenants to. Either way, the size is printed on the existing filter.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Mobile and Manufactured Homes</h3>

          <p>
            Mobile home furnaces often take filters in less common sizes or in a slot inside the furnace cabinet itself. Read the size on the old filter or in the furnace manual, and choose a moderate MERV 8 or 11, because compact systems can be sensitive to airflow restriction.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When the Filter Is Hard to Reach</h3>

          <p>
            If the filter is awkward to get to, it is easy to forget. Set phone reminders or use the Filtrete Smart App, and keep the next filter stored right beside the slot.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            14. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the Wrong Size</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Sizes are printed on the old filter's frame. A 16x25x1 is not the same as a 16x25x4 or a 16x20x1. Buy the exact size printed on your current filter, and do not trim or force a filter to fit.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing the Highest MERV Without Checking the System</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A MERV 13 filter can strain an older or undersized system. If your HVAC manual recommends a lower rating, or the system struggles after you upgrade, step back to MERV 11.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting to Change It</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A clogged filter restricts airflow more than any MERV rating and makes the furnace work harder. Set reminders, check monthly and change at least every 90 days.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Installing It Backwards</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The airflow arrow must point toward the furnace. A backwards filter filters poorly and can collapse.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting a Furnace Filter to Replace an Air Purifier</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 1-inch filter only cleans air passing through the system. For serious allergies, smoke or odors, add a HEPA air purifier in the rooms where you spend the most time.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

