        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            15. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best furnace filter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most homes, the Filtrete MPR 1000 MERV 11 is the best furnace filter. It captures pet dander and fine dust without straining a typical system, is certified asthma and allergy friendly per its listing, and works with an app for change reminders. Choose MERV 13, like the Filtrete MPR 1900 or Aerostar, for allergies if your system can handle it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What MERV rating is best for a furnace filter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                MERV 11 is the best balance for most homes: much better dust and dander capture than MERV 8 without much airflow penalty. MERV 13 suits allergy sufferers and smoke where the system can handle it. MERV 8 is fine for homes without allergies or pets. Check your HVAC manual before going above MERV 11.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a higher MERV filter bad for my furnace?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not usually, if your system is designed for it and you change the filter on time. Higher MERV filters restrict airflow more, which can strain older or undersized systems. A clogged filter of any rating is worse than a clean high-MERV one. Ask a technician if your system struggles.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should I change my furnace filter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most 1-inch pleated filters are rated for up to about 90 days. Change every 30 to 60 days with pets, allergies, smokers or heavy use. Check monthly by holding the filter up to the light, and change it if light no longer passes through.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best furnace filter for allergies?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A MERV 13 filter, like the Filtrete MPR 1900 or Aerostar MERV 13, captures the finest allergens a 1-inch filter can. The Filtrete MPR 1900 lists a 62 percent capture rate for 0.3 to 1 micron particles. Pair it with a HEPA air purifier in the bedroom for the best results.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are washable furnace filters worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They can be if you dislike buying filters and will wash them on schedule. The K&N MERV 11 is rated equal to good disposable filters, and K&N lists about $30 a year in savings. The catch is drying time: the filter must be fully dry before refitting, so many owners keep a spare.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which way does the arrow go on a furnace filter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The airflow arrow points toward the furnace, in the direction air travels, away from the return duct. Installing it backwards reduces filtration and can damage the filter. Check before closing the slot.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

